- FIRST REAL RUN ON gpt-4.1 (user, other machine): a KPI+chart+narrative slide
  came out BADLY - KPI cards h=320 with justifyContent="spaceBetween" (label
  glued top / number floating low / delta clipped), title overlapping the cards,
  chart overlapping the KPI deltas. REPRODUCED here exactly. Root cause: the
  grammar-only approach lost the concrete KPI example the OLD pipeline injected
  (kpi-slide.xml), and gpt-4.1 (a) rebuilt "KPI tile = h=320 card" from training
  bias and (b) did NOT do the band-height arithmetic -> band heights summed to
  ~1000 in a 720 box -> POM's default flexShrink=1 silently squashed and
  overlapped everything, NO compile error. User's "lack of examples" hypothesis
  was correct. FIX (committed after b6c9109):
  * NEW src/knowledge/core/recipes.yaml - compiled copy-the-shape snippets
    (kpi_row, chart_card, table_card, bullet_list, callout) with SANE band
    heights. context_builder._KIND_TO_RECIPE injects only the ones this slide
    needs, as contract["component_recipes"], rendered as "## COMPONENT RECIPES".
  * house-style.yaml: FLEX SHRINK is now render-gotcha #1; height_budget rewritten
    ("total must be <=700, over 720 WILL overlap"); explicit typical band heights;
    kpi_tile guidance (h 110-150, justifyContent center never spaceBetween).
  * _select_notes gained has_grammar flag - skips design-language card/chart/
    bullet rules for standard/dense (grammar+recipes cover them) to make room.
  * layout_audit: NEW BAND_HEIGHT_SUM high-severity check - sums root-VStack
    child heights + gaps + padding, flags >760. Catches the flexShrink overlap
    that the POM compiler does not. Feeds render_check + the critic.
  * prompt budgets: standard ~5.4k, dense ~6.3k; test thresholds raised to
    5700 / 6600 with rationale (recipes are load-bearing). 306 unit tests pass.
  LESSON: grammar-first is right for composition; gpt-4.1 needs concrete
  compiled recipes for fiddly components. The render->critic->repair loop
  (user-deferred) would have caught this on the first run - worth revisiting.
